<?xml version='1.0' standalone='yes'?>

<!DOCTYPE PLUGIN [
<!ENTITY name      "failover-guard">
<!ENTITY author    "Nebur692">
<!ENTITY version   "2026.08.21.1">
<!ENTITY launch    "Settings/FailoverGuard">
<!ENTITY gitURL    "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nebur692/failover-guard-unraid-plugin/main">
<!ENTITY pluginURL "&gitURL;/&name;.plg">
<!ENTITY plugdir   "/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;">
<!ENTITY cfgdir    "/boot/config/plugins/&name;">
]>

<PLUGIN name="&name;" author="&author;" version="&version;" launch="&launch;"
        pluginURL="&pluginURL;"
        support="https://github.com/Nebur692/failover-guard-unraid-plugin/issues"
        min="6.12.0">

<CHANGES>
###2026.08.21.1
- The standby copy is watched too. Until now the only thing ever checked about
  plan B was that a DNS record resolved, so a replica could sit for days
  answering errors and nothing noticed until the day it had to take over — which
  is exactly what happened here. Set TARGET_HEALTH_URL to a URL where the copy on
  this box answers (the backend directly: at rest the proxy host is disabled, so
  the site is not reachable locally by its public name) and it is asked every
  TARGET_CHECK_INTERVAL. Its state shows next to the origin on the page, and only
  changes are notified.
- A broken standby warns, it never vetoes. Refusing to switch would turn a
  degraded site into an offline one, and this probe can be wrong too; the failover
  goes ahead with a loud notification instead.
- A failover target whose address does not match this box's public IP no longer
  blocks the switch on its own when the standby answers. On a box behind two
  internet lines there is no single "our public IP", and the line traffic leaves
  by is not necessarily the one the record points at.

###2026.08.21
- The settings page never saved anything. The form posted to /update.htm, which
  is only the progress page; the file is written by /update.php. Every field
  looked like it was applied and nothing reached the config file.
- The watcher read the configuration once at start and never again, so a change
  made on the page did nothing until someone restarted it. It is now reloaded
  whenever the file changes, and the reload is logged.
- A broken resolver was reported as a dead origin. Every probe reaches the origin
  by name, but the "do we have internet" check only opened a socket to an IP, so
  with DNS down all of them failed and the origin was declared dead. That check
  now also requires a name to resolve, and returns "inconclusive" instead.
- Failures are ignored for the first few minutes after this box boots
  (BOOT_GRACE, 300s). Right after a reboot the network stack and Docker are still
  coming up and anything measured says more about us than about the origin: a
  real reboot produced four failover attempts in six minutes.

###2026.08.20.8
- The watcher could die together with the shell that started it: setsid only
  creates a new session when the caller is not already a process group leader, so
  starting it by hand from a terminal left it attached to that terminal. Started
  from cron it survived, which is why it went unnoticed. It now always forks.

###2026.08.20.7
- A hook that refuses to run (because the site is busy and copying would capture
  a half-written state) left the copy pending, which meant it was retried on
  every pass of the watcher — a connection to the origin every few seconds for as
  long as the site stayed busy. There is now a configurable wait between retries,
  60 seconds by default.

###2026.08.20.6
- The per-field help is back to Unraid's own mechanism: plain blockquotes that
  the built-in Help button in the top bar unfolds, instead of the custom question
  mark widget added in .5, which was not how Unraid does this.

###2026.08.20.5
- The origin health is now the first and biggest thing on the page, on the same
  row as the rest of the state instead of dropped onto its own line below it.
- Every field has a "?" next to its label that unfolds a plain-language
  explanation of what it does and what happens if you get it wrong. The previous
  notes only showed up if you found the global help toggle, and several fields
  had no explanation at all.

###2026.08.20.4
- The settings page now shows what the watcher is seeing: origin healthy, origin
  down, or inconclusive because this server itself has no internet, plus the
  consecutive failure count and how many seconds ago it was checked. Until now
  that only appeared if you pressed the check button. The value is published by
  the watcher rather than probed on page load, so the page is instant and always
  agrees with what the watcher is acting on, and it is shown as "no recent
  reading" if the watcher stopped updating it.

###2026.08.20.3
- The "enabled" line in the checks output showed the raw yes/no from the config
  file instead of the translated word.

###2026.08.20.2
- The settings page, the checks output and the notifications now follow the
  language selected in Unraid (Settings -> Display Settings), instead of showing
  both languages at once. Spanish when the locale starts with "es", English
  otherwise, so any other language still gets a readable page. The log file
  stays in English on purpose: it is what gets pasted into a bug report.

###2026.08.20.1
- The settings page now shows the log: last 100 to 2000 lines, with refresh,
  clear, and an optional 5 second auto-refresh that keeps the newest lines in
  view. Until now the page only told you where the file was.

###2026.08.20
- First release. Watches a remote origin from your Unraid box and fails over
  automatically when it dies: switches the Cloudflare DNS records of a zone to a
  failover target and enables the matching Nginx Proxy Manager host, then returns
  everything when the origin comes back.
- Detection in about interval x threshold seconds (10s x 3 by default), not the
  several minutes a cron job would take.
- Three guards against false positives: a failure is not counted when this
  server itself has no internet, an open port alone is not accepted as healthy
  when a health path is configured, and a configurable number of consecutive
  failures is required before anything is switched.
- Refuses to switch when the failover target is not reachable or its dynamic DNS
  has not caught up with the current public IP, since sending users nowhere is
  worse than leaving the outage alone.
- Your data is moved by your own hook scripts, never by the plugin. The
  pre-failback hook runs with the local host already disabled, so nothing can be
  written while the copy is in flight, and a failing hook aborts the failback
  instead of losing whatever arrived during the outage.
</CHANGES>

<FILE Name="/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/failover-guard/scripts/failover-guard" Mode="0755">
 <INLINE>
<![CDATA[
#!/bin/bash
# failover-guard — watches a remote origin and fails over to this Unraid box.
#
# Modes:
#   watch     permanent loop, checks the origin every CHECK_INTERVAL seconds
#   failover  switch DNS to the failover target and enable the NPM host
#   failback  return DNS to the origin and disable the NPM host
#   status    print current state as JSON (used by the settings page)
#   test      run every check once and report, without changing anything
#
# The rule that governs the whole design: at any moment only ONE side accepts
# writes. Data always moves from that side to the other, never both ways. The
# plugin itself never touches your data — it calls your hook scripts for that,
# because only you know what "your data" means.

set -o pipefail

PLUGIN="failover-guard"
CONFIG="/boot/config/plugins/$PLUGIN/config"
STATE="/boot/config/plugins/$PLUGIN/state"
LOG="/var/log/$PLUGIN.log"

mkdir -p "$STATE"

# ---- Defaults, overridden by the config file ----------------------------

ENABLED="no"
ORIGIN_HOST=""                # host name of the remote origin (not the domain)
ORIGIN_PORTS="443"            # space separated TCP ports that prove it is alive
HEALTH_PATH=""                # optional: HTTP path on the origin, e.g. /api/health
HEALTH_EXPECT=""              # optional: string the response must contain
DOMAIN=""                     # Cloudflare zone / domain to switch
FAILOVER_TARGET=""            # CNAME or IP that points at this box
CF_TOKEN=""
CHECK_INTERVAL=10
FAIL_THRESHOLD=3
NPM_DATA=""                   # e.g. /mnt/cache/appdata/NginxProxyManager/data
NPM_CONTAINER="Nginx-Proxy-Manager-Official"
NPM_HOST_ID=""
TARGET_HEALTH_URL=""          # optional: URL of the standby copy ON THIS BOX
TARGET_EXPECT=""              # optional: string its response must contain
TARGET_CHECK_INTERVAL=300     # how often to ask the standby whether it still works
HOOK_REPLICATE=""             # runs while the origin is healthy: origin -> here
HOOK_PRE_FAILBACK=""          # runs before returning: here -> origin
REPLICATE_INTERVAL=3600
RETRY_INTERVAL=60             # wait this long before retrying a hook that refused
BOOT_GRACE=300                # ignore failures for this long after this box booted
NOTIFY="yes"

[ -f "$CONFIG" ] && . "$CONFIG"

# The watcher is a long-lived process: without this it would keep serving the
# settings it was started with, and a change made on the page would silently do
# nothing until someone happened to restart it. update.php writes the file
# atomically, so sourcing it mid-flight cannot read half a file.
CONFIG_STAMP=$(stat -c %Y "$CONFIG" 2>/dev/null)
reload_config_if_changed() {
    local stamp
    stamp=$(stat -c %Y "$CONFIG" 2>/dev/null)
    [ -z "$stamp" ] && return 1
    [ "$stamp" = "$CONFIG_STAMP" ] && return 1
    CONFIG_STAMP="$stamp"
    . "$CONFIG"
    log "Configuration reloaded"
    return 0
}

MODE="${1:-status}"

# ---- Helpers ------------------------------------------------------------

log() { echo "$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S') $*" >> "$LOG"; }

# Follows the language picked in Unraid, for the things a person reads: the
# checks output and the notifications. The log itself stays in English on
# purpose — it is what gets pasted into a bug report.
DYN_LOCALE=$(grep -oP '(?<=^locale=")[^"]*' /boot/config/plugins/dynamix/dynamix.cfg 2>/dev/null)
case "$DYN_LOCALE" in es*) LANG_ES=1 ;; *) LANG_ES=0 ;; esac
tt() { [ "$LANG_ES" = "1" ] && echo "$2" || echo "$1"; }

notify() {
    [ "$NOTIFY" = "yes" ] || return 0
    local subject="$1" text="$2" icon="${3:-warning}"
    [ -x /usr/local/emhttp/webGui/scripts/notify ] && \
        /usr/local/emhttp/webGui/scripts/notify -e "Failover Guard" \
            -s "$subject" -d "$text" -i "$icon" >/dev/null 2>&1
}

cf_api() {
    local method="$1" path="$2" data="$3"
    if [ -n "$data" ]; then
        curl -s -X "$method" -H "Authorization: Bearer $CF_TOKEN" \
             -H "Content-Type: application/json" --data "$data" \
             "https://api.cloudflare.com/client/v4$path"
    else
        curl -s -X "$method" -H "Authorization: Bearer $CF_TOKEN" \
             "https://api.cloudflare.com/client/v4$path"
    fi
}

cf_zone() { cf_api GET "/zones?name=$DOMAIN" | jq -r '.result[0].id'; }

# ---- Health checks ------------------------------------------------------

# Does this box have internet at all? If not, the origin may be perfectly fine
# and we simply cannot see it — and failing over would be pointless anyway,
# since nobody could reach us either.
#
# Reaching an IP is not enough: every probe below reaches the origin BY NAME, so
# a resolver that is not up yet makes them all fail and the origin gets declared
# dead while it is perfectly healthy. Well known names are used on purpose, never
# the origin's own: if the origin's record really disappeared that is a genuine
# outage, not an excuse to look the other way.
local_net_ok() {
    local d reachable=0
    for d in 1.1.1.1 8.8.8.8; do
        timeout 3 bash -c "echo > /dev/tcp/$d/443" 2>/dev/null && { reachable=1; break; }
    done
    [ "$reachable" = "1" ] || return 1
    for d in one.one.one.one dns.google; do
        [ -n "$(timeout 5 getent hosts "$d" 2>/dev/null | head -1)" ] && return 0
    done
    return 1
}

# Right after a reboot the network stack, the resolver and Docker are still
# coming up, so anything we measure says more about us than about the origin.
just_booted() {
    local up
    [ "${BOOT_GRACE:-0}" -gt 0 ] 2>/dev/null || return 1
    up=$(cut -d. -f1 /proc/uptime 2>/dev/null)
    [ -n "$up" ] && [ "$up" -lt "$BOOT_GRACE" ]
}

origin_ports_open() {
    local p
    for p in $ORIGIN_PORTS; do
        timeout 6 bash -c "echo > /dev/tcp/$ORIGIN_HOST/$p" 2>/dev/null && return 0
    done
    return 1
}

# An open port only proves the machine booted. If HEALTH_PATH is set we ask the
# application itself, hitting the origin by IP so we bypass any CDN in front —
# otherwise the CDN would answer happily with the origin long dead.
origin_app_ok() {
    [ -z "$HEALTH_PATH" ] && return 0
    local ip body
    ip=$(getent hosts "$ORIGIN_HOST" | awk '{print $1}' | head -1)
    [ -z "$ip" ] && return 1
    body=$(timeout 8 curl -s --max-time 8 --resolve "$DOMAIN:443:$ip" \
           "https://$DOMAIN$HEALTH_PATH" 2>/dev/null)
    [ -z "$HEALTH_EXPECT" ] && { [ -n "$body" ] && return 0 || return 1; }
    case "$body" in *"$HEALTH_EXPECT"*) return 0 ;; *) return 1 ;; esac
}

# 0 = healthy · 1 = down · 2 = inconclusive (our own network is broken)
origin_health() {
    local_net_ok || return 2
    origin_ports_open || return 1
    origin_app_ok || return 1
    return 0
}

# Is the standby copy on this box actually able to serve? Everything else here
# watches the origin; without this the only thing ever checked about plan B is
# that a DNS record resolves. A replica can sit there for days answering errors
# and nothing notices until the day it has to take over.
#
# It has to be asked at a URL that works while the copy is at rest: if the proxy
# host is disabled (which is the correct resting state) the site is not reachable
# by its public name from here, so this points straight at the backend.
# 0 = healthy · 1 = not healthy · 2 = not configured
target_app_ok() {
    [ -z "$TARGET_HEALTH_URL" ] && return 2
    local body
    body=$(timeout 10 curl -s --max-time 10 "$TARGET_HEALTH_URL" 2>/dev/null) || return 1
    [ -z "$TARGET_EXPECT" ] && { [ -n "$body" ] && return 0 || return 1; }
    case "$body" in *"$TARGET_EXPECT"*) return 0 ;; *) return 1 ;; esac
}

# Never send traffic to a failover target that is not ready: if your dynamic DNS
# has not caught up, switching sends users nowhere. Worse than staying put.
target_ready() {
    [ -z "$FAILOVER_TARGET" ] && return 1
    case "$FAILOVER_TARGET" in
        *[a-zA-Z]*)
            local ip_t ip_w
            ip_t=$(getent hosts "$FAILOVER_TARGET" | awk '{print $1}' | head -1)
            [ -z "$ip_t" ] && return 1
            ip_w=$(timeout 8 curl -s https://api.ipify.org 2>/dev/null)
            [ -z "$ip_w" ] && return 0
            [ "$ip_t" = "$ip_w" ] && return 0
            # A mismatch used to be an outright veto. It cannot be, on a box
            # behind more than one internet line: there is no single "our public
            # IP" then, and the line traffic happens to leave by is not
            # necessarily the one the record points at. So when the standby copy
            # itself answers, that is better evidence than the address
            # comparison and it wins.
            if target_app_ok; then
                log "Failover target $FAILOVER_TARGET resolves to $ip_t and traffic leaves through $ip_w, but the standby copy answers: switching anyway"
                return 0
            fi
            log "Failover target $FAILOVER_TARGET resolves to $ip_t but our public IP is $ip_w"
            return 1 ;;
        *) return 0 ;;
    esac
}

# ---- Nginx Proxy Manager ------------------------------------------------
#
# Done the same way NPM does it internally: a disabled host keeps its .conf
# with only the comment header. No API credentials needed.

npm_conf() { echo "$NPM_DATA/nginx/proxy_host/${NPM_HOST_ID}.conf"; }
npm_db()   { echo "$NPM_DATA/database.sqlite"; }

npm_configured() { [ -n "$NPM_DATA" ] && [ -n "$NPM_HOST_ID" ] && [ -f "$(npm_db)" ]; }

npm_state() {
    npm_configured || { echo "n/a"; return; }
    sqlite3 "$(npm_db)" "SELECT enabled FROM proxy_host WHERE id=$NPM_HOST_ID;" 2>/dev/null
}

npm_enable() {
    npm_configured || return 0
    [ "$(npm_state)" = "1" ] && return 0
    if [ ! -s "$STATE/npm_host.conf" ]; then
        log "No saved copy of the NPM host config; cannot enable it"
        return 1
    fi
    cp "$STATE/npm_host.conf" "$(npm_conf)"
    sqlite3 "$(npm_db)" "UPDATE proxy_host SET enabled=1 WHERE id=$NPM_HOST_ID;"
    docker exec "$NPM_CONTAINER" nginx -s reload >/dev/null 2>&1
    log "NPM host $NPM_HOST_ID enabled"
}

npm_disable() {
    npm_configured || return 0
    # Save the good config first: it is the only thing that lets us turn it back on.
    [ -s "$(npm_conf)" ] && [ "$(wc -l < "$(npm_conf)")" -gt 4 ] && cp "$(npm_conf)" "$STATE/npm_host.conf"
    [ "$(npm_state)" = "0" ] && return 0
    printf '# ------------------------------------------------------------\n# %s\n# ------------------------------------------------------------\n' "$DOMAIN" > "$(npm_conf)"
    sqlite3 "$(npm_db)" "UPDATE proxy_host SET enabled=0 WHERE id=$NPM_HOST_ID;"
    docker exec "$NPM_CONTAINER" nginx -s reload >/dev/null 2>&1
    log "NPM host $NPM_HOST_ID disabled"
}

# ---- DNS ----------------------------------------------------------------

# Points every A/CNAME record of the zone at the given destination, keeping the
# proxied flag each record already had.
dns_point_to() {
    local dest="$1" type zone records id name proxied res ok=0 bad=0
    case "$dest" in *[a-zA-Z]*) type="CNAME" ;; *) type="A" ;; esac

    zone=$(cf_zone)
    if [ -z "$zone" ] || [ "$zone" = "null" ]; then
        log "Cloudflare: could not read zone for $DOMAIN"
        return 1
    fi
    records=$(cf_api GET "/zones/$zone/dns_records" \
              | jq -r '.result[] | select(.type=="A" or .type=="CNAME") | "\(.id)|\(.name)|\(.proxied)"')

    while IFS='|' read -r id name proxied; do
        [ -z "$id" ] && continue
        res=$(cf_api PUT "/zones/$zone/dns_records/$id" \
              "{\"type\":\"$type\",\"name\":\"$name\",\"content\":\"$dest\",\"proxied\":$proxied,\"ttl\":1}" \
              | jq -r .success)
        if [ "$res" = "true" ]; then log "DNS: $name -> $type $dest"; ok=$((ok+1))
        else log "DNS: failed to update $name"; bad=$((bad+1)); fi
    done <<< "$records"

    [ $ok -gt 0 ] && [ $bad -eq 0 ]
}

origin_ip() { getent hosts "$ORIGIN_HOST" | awk '{print $1}' | head -1; }

# ---- Actions ------------------------------------------------------------

run_hook() {
    local hook="$1" label="$2"
    [ -z "$hook" ] && return 0
    [ -f "$hook" ] || { log "Hook $label not found: $hook"; return 1; }
    log "Running $label hook: $hook"
    if bash "$hook" >> "$LOG" 2>&1; then
        log "$label hook finished OK"; return 0
    fi
    log "$label hook FAILED"; return 1
}

do_failover() {
    [ -f "$STATE/switched" ] && return 0
    log "FAILOVER: origin is down, serving from this box"

    if ! target_ready; then
        log "Failover target is not ready; refusing to switch"
        notify "$(tt 'Failover NOT performed' 'Failover NO ejecutado')" "$(tt 'The failover target is not reachable or your dynamic DNS is stale.' 'El destino del failover no es alcanzable o tu DNS dinámico está desactualizado.')" "alert"
        return 1
    fi

    # A standby that answers wrongly is a warning, never a veto. Refusing here
    # would turn "the site is degraded" into "the site is down", and this probe
    # can be wrong too. The decision belongs to whoever reads the alert.
    if ! target_app_ok && [ -n "$TARGET_HEALTH_URL" ]; then
        log "WARNING: the standby copy is not answering as expected; switching anyway, but check it now"
        notify "$(tt 'Failover onto a copy that looks broken' 'Failover a una copia que parece rota')" \
               "$(tt "Switching because the origin is down, but $TARGET_HEALTH_URL is not answering as expected. Check the site." "Se conmuta porque el origen está caído, pero $TARGET_HEALTH_URL no responde como se espera. Revisa el sitio.")" "alert"
    fi

    # Enable locally first: switching DNS onto a box that does not serve the
    # domain yet would hand users an error.
    npm_enable || { notify "$(tt 'Failover failed' 'Failover fallido')" "$(tt 'Could not enable the NPM host.' 'No se pudo habilitar el host de NPM.')" "alert"; return 1; }

    if dns_point_to "$FAILOVER_TARGET"; then
        date +%s > "$STATE/switched"
        notify "$(tt 'Failover performed' 'Failover ejecutado')" "$(tt "$DOMAIN now points to $FAILOVER_TARGET and is served from this server." "$DOMAIN ahora apunta a $FAILOVER_TARGET y se sirve desde este servidor.")" "alert"
        return 0
    fi

    npm_disable
    notify "$(tt 'Failover failed' 'Failover fallido')" "$(tt 'DNS could not be updated; the local host was left disabled so two copies never serve at once.' 'No se pudo cambiar el DNS; el host local se dejó deshabilitado para que nunca sirvan dos copias a la vez.')" "alert"
    return 1
}

do_failback() {
    [ -f "$STATE/switched" ] || return 0
    local ip
    ip=$(origin_ip)
    [ -z "$ip" ] && { log "Cannot resolve the origin; failback aborted"; return 1; }

    log "FAILBACK: origin is back. Freezing this box before moving data"

    # Freeze first: from here on nobody writes on this side, so whatever the
    # hook uploads is the complete, final picture.
    npm_disable

    if ! run_hook "$HOOK_PRE_FAILBACK" "pre-failback"; then
        log "Data hook failed; keeping this box in service"
        npm_enable
        notify "$(tt 'Failback aborted' 'Failback abortado')" "$(tt 'The pre-failback hook failed, so we keep serving locally rather than lose data.' 'El enganche previo al failback falló, así que se sigue sirviendo en local en vez de perder datos.')" "alert"
        return 1
    fi

    if dns_point_to "$ip"; then
        rm -f "$STATE/switched"
        date +%s > "$STATE/last_replicate"
        notify "$(tt 'Failback complete' 'Failback completado')" "$(tt "$DOMAIN is served from the origin again ($ip). The local host is disabled." "$DOMAIN vuelve a servirse desde el origen ($ip). El host local queda deshabilitado.")" "normal"
        return 0
    fi

    log "DNS could not be updated; keeping this box in service"
    npm_enable
    return 1
}

maybe_replicate() {
    [ -z "$HOOK_REPLICATE" ] && return 0
    local now prev att
    now=$(date +%s)

    # Time for the next scheduled copy?
    if [ -f "$STATE/last_replicate" ]; then
        prev=$(cat "$STATE/last_replicate")
        [ $((now - prev)) -lt "$REPLICATE_INTERVAL" ] && return 0
    fi

    # A hook is allowed to refuse: the usual reason is that the site is busy right
    # now and copying would capture a half-written state. When it refuses, the
    # success mark is deliberately not updated, so the copy stays due — but
    # without this backoff "still due" would mean retrying on every single pass of
    # the watcher, which is every few seconds, hammering the origin for as long as
    # the site stays busy.
    if [ -f "$STATE/last_attempt" ]; then
        att=$(cat "$STATE/last_attempt")
        [ $((now - att)) -lt "$RETRY_INTERVAL" ] && return 0
    fi
    echo "$now" > "$STATE/last_attempt"

    if run_hook "$HOOK_REPLICATE" "replicate"; then
        echo "$now" > "$STATE/last_replicate"
    fi
}

# ---- Watch loop ---------------------------------------------------------

# The settings page reads this instead of running its own check: it shows what
# the watcher actually sees, and costs nothing to display.
publish_health() {
    printf 'health=%s\nfails=%s\nchecked=%s\ntarget=%s\n' \
           "$1" "$2" "$(date +%s)" "$(cat "$STATE/target_health" 2>/dev/null || echo off)" \
           > "$STATE/health"
}

# Checked on its own slower clock, not on every pass: the standby breaking is a
# slow kind of accident (a bad sync, a permissions change), and there is no point
# knocking on it every few seconds. Only transitions are announced, so a copy
# that stays broken does not turn into a notification every five minutes.
check_target() {
    local now state prev
    now=$(date +%s)
    [ -z "$TARGET_HEALTH_URL" ] && { echo off > "$STATE/target_health"; return 0; }
    [ $((now - ${last_target_check:-0})) -lt "$TARGET_CHECK_INTERVAL" ] && return 0
    last_target_check="$now"

    target_app_ok && state=ok || state=fail
    prev=$(cat "$STATE/target_health" 2>/dev/null)
    echo "$state" > "$STATE/target_health"
    [ "$state" = "$prev" ] && return 0

    if [ "$state" = "fail" ]; then
        log "STANDBY COPY UNHEALTHY: $TARGET_HEALTH_URL is not answering as expected"
        notify "$(tt 'Standby copy is broken' 'La copia en espera está rota')" \
               "$(tt "The copy on this box answered wrongly at $TARGET_HEALTH_URL. A failover right now would serve a broken site." "La copia de este servidor respondió mal en $TARGET_HEALTH_URL. Un failover ahora serviría un sitio roto.")" "alert"
    elif [ -n "$prev" ] && [ "$prev" != "off" ]; then
        log "Standby copy is healthy again"
        notify "$(tt 'Standby copy is healthy again' 'La copia en espera vuelve a estar sana')" \
               "$(tt "$TARGET_HEALTH_URL answers as expected again." "$TARGET_HEALTH_URL vuelve a responder como se espera.")" "normal"
    fi
}

watch_loop() {
    local fails=0 st booting=0 last_target_check=0
    log "Watcher started (every ${CHECK_INTERVAL}s, $FAIL_THRESHOLD failures to switch)"
    echo $$ > "$STATE/watcher.pid"
    trap 'rm -f "$STATE/watcher.pid"; exit 0' TERM INT

    while true; do
        reload_config_if_changed
        if [ "$ENABLED" != "yes" ]; then sleep "$CHECK_INTERVAL"; continue; fi

        if just_booted; then
            [ "$booting" != "1" ] && { log "This box booted less than ${BOOT_GRACE}s ago: not judging the origin yet"; booting=1; }
            publish_health 2 0; fails=0
            sleep "$CHECK_INTERVAL"; continue
        fi
        booting=0

        # Watched even while switched over: after a failover the standby IS the
        # live site, and it is exactly then that knowing it works matters most.
        # Kept after the boot grace on purpose — a copy whose container is still
        # starting is not a broken copy.
        check_target

        origin_health; st=$?
        publish_health "$st" "$fails"

        if [ -f "$STATE/switched" ]; then
            # Already switched: what we are waiting for now is the origin to
            # come back, and then the failback.
            if [ $st -eq 0 ]; then
                fails=$((fails+1))
                if [ $fails -ge $FAIL_THRESHOLD ]; then do_failback; fails=0; sleep 60; fi
            else
                fails=0
            fi
            sleep "$CHECK_INTERVAL"; continue
        fi

        case $st in
            0) [ $fails -gt 0 ] && log "Origin responding again (discarding $fails failures)"
               fails=0
               maybe_replicate ;;
            2) [ $fails -gt 0 ] && log "No working internet or DNS on this box: not counting the failure"
               fails=0 ;;
            1) fails=$((fails+1))
               log "Origin not responding (failure $fails of $FAIL_THRESHOLD)"
               if [ $fails -ge $FAIL_THRESHOLD ]; then
                   if [ -n "$HOOK_REPLICATE" ] && [ ! -f "$STATE/last_replicate" ]; then
                       log "No replica has ever run: refusing to switch to an empty copy"
                   else
                       do_failover
                   fi
                   fails=0; sleep 60
               fi ;;
        esac
        sleep "$CHECK_INTERVAL"
    done
}

watcher_running() {
    local pid
    pid=$(cat "$STATE/watcher.pid" 2>/dev/null)
    [ -n "$pid" ] && kill -0 "$pid" 2>/dev/null
}

# ---- Entry point --------------------------------------------------------

case "$MODE" in
    watch)    watch_loop ;;
    failover) do_failover ;;
    failback) do_failback ;;
    replicate) run_hook "$HOOK_REPLICATE" "replicate" && date +%s > "$STATE/last_replicate" ;;
    test)
        origin_health; h=$?
        w=22
        printf "%-${w}s %s\n" "$(tt 'enabled:' 'activado:')" \
               "$([ "$ENABLED" = "yes" ] && tt yes sí || tt no no)"
        printf "%-${w}s %s\n" "$(tt 'local network and DNS:' 'red local y DNS:')" \
               "$(local_net_ok && echo OK || echo "$(tt FAIL FALLO)")"
        printf "%-${w}s %s\n" "$(tt 'origin ports:' 'puertos del origen:')" \
               "$(origin_ports_open && echo OK || echo "$(tt FAIL FALLO)")"
        printf "%-${w}s %s\n" "$(tt 'origin app:' 'aplicación del origen:')" \
               "$([ -z "$HEALTH_PATH" ] && tt 'not configured' 'sin configurar' || (origin_app_ok && echo OK || echo "$(tt FAIL FALLO)"))"
        printf "%-${w}s %s\n" "$(tt 'origin health:' 'salud del origen:')" \
               "$([ $h -eq 0 ] && tt HEALTHY SANO || ([ $h -eq 2 ] && tt INCONCLUSIVE 'NO CONCLUYENTE' || tt DOWN CAÍDO))"
        printf "%-${w}s %s\n" "$(tt 'target ready:' 'destino listo:')" \
               "$(target_ready && echo OK || echo "$(tt FAIL FALLO)")"
        target_app_ok; ta=$?
        printf "%-${w}s %s\n" "$(tt 'standby copy:' 'copia en espera:')" \
               "$([ $ta -eq 2 ] && tt 'not configured' 'sin configurar' || { [ $ta -eq 0 ] && tt HEALTHY SANA || tt BROKEN ROTA; })"
        printf "%-${w}s %s\n" "$(tt 'npm host:' 'host de NPM:')" \
               "$(s=$(npm_state); [ "$s" = "1" ] && tt 'enabled' 'habilitado' || { [ "$s" = "0" ] && tt 'disabled' 'deshabilitado' || echo "$s"; })"
        printf "%-${w}s %s\n" "$(tt 'switched:' 'conmutado:')" \
               "$([ -f "$STATE/switched" ] && tt yes sí || tt no no)"
        printf "%-${w}s %s\n" "$(tt 'watcher:' 'vigilante:')" \
               "$(watcher_running && tt running 'en marcha' || tt stopped parado)"
        ;;
    status)
        health=""; fails=""; checked=""; target=""
        [ -f "$STATE/health" ] && . "$STATE/health"
        jq -n --arg enabled "$ENABLED" \
              --arg target "${target:-off}" \
              --arg watcher "$(watcher_running && echo running || echo stopped)" \
              --arg switched "$([ -f "$STATE/switched" ] && echo yes || echo no)" \
              --arg npm "$(npm_state)" \
              --arg last "$(cat "$STATE/last_replicate" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)" \
              --arg health "${health:-}" --arg fails "${fails:-0}" --arg checked "${checked:-0}" \
              '{enabled:$enabled,watcher:$watcher,switched:$switched,npm_host:$npm,
                last_replicate:$last,health:$health,fails:$fails,checked:$checked,
                target:$target}'
        ;;
    *) echo "usage: $0 {watch|failover|failback|replicate|test|status}"; exit 1 ;;
esac
]]>
 </INLINE>
 <SHA256>a63fa1e7e5eba930ab3dd0142e675ac8fc14554b0968a58d4ca479ca9f72f889</SHA256>
</FILE>

<FILE Name="/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/failover-guard/scripts/supervise" Mode="0755">
 <INLINE>
<![CDATA[
#!/bin/bash
# Keeps the watcher alive. Runs from cron every few minutes, so the watcher
# survives a crash, an array restart or a plugin update without anyone noticing.

PLUGIN="failover-guard"
BASE="/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/$PLUGIN"
STATE="/boot/config/plugins/$PLUGIN/state"
CONFIG="/boot/config/plugins/$PLUGIN/config"
LOG="/var/log/$PLUGIN.log"

ENABLED="no"
[ -f "$CONFIG" ] && . "$CONFIG"

mkdir -p "$STATE"

pid=$(cat "$STATE/watcher.pid" 2>/dev/null)

if [ "$ENABLED" != "yes" ]; then
    # Disabled on purpose: stop the watcher if it is still around.
    if [ -n "$pid" ] && kill -0 "$pid" 2>/dev/null; then
        kill "$pid" 2>/dev/null
        echo "$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S') Watcher stopped (plugin disabled)" >> "$LOG"
    fi
    rm -f "$STATE/watcher.pid"
    exit 0
fi

if [ -n "$pid" ] && kill -0 "$pid" 2>/dev/null; then exit 0; fi

# --fork is not optional: without it setsid only creates a new session when the
# caller is not already a process group leader, so launching this from an
# interactive shell leaves the watcher in that shell's group and it dies with it.
# Started from cron it happened to survive, which is exactly the kind of bug that
# only shows up for whoever runs it by hand.
setsid --fork bash "$BASE/scripts/failover-guard" watch >/dev/null 2>&1 < /dev/null &
echo "$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S') Watcher started by supervisor" >> "$LOG"
]]>
 </INLINE>
 <SHA256>751dbea6c51e6a08669bf243f61a707cce81f725dd463df1301363e4d1c1b8e8</SHA256>
</FILE>

<FILE Name="/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/failover-guard/FailoverGuard.page" Mode="0644">
 <INLINE>
<![CDATA[
Menu="Utilities"
Title="Failover Guard"
Icon="fa-exchange"
---
<?php
/* Failover Guard — settings page.
   The page follows the language picked in Unraid (Settings -> Display Settings),
   read straight from dynamix.cfg. Spanish if the locale starts with "es",
   English otherwise, so any other language falls back to something readable
   rather than to an empty page. */

$plugin  = "failover-guard";
$cfgfile = "/boot/config/plugins/$plugin/config";
$script  = "/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/$plugin/scripts/failover-guard";

$dyn    = @parse_ini_file("/boot/config/plugins/dynamix/dynamix.cfg");
$locale = $dyn['locale'] ?? '';
$ES     = (stripos($locale, 'es') === 0);

/* t() picks the string for the active language. Kept deliberately simple: a
   full language-pack integration would mean shipping .txt files that only get
   loaded when the matching pack is installed, and this page has to work either
   way. */
function t($en, $es) { global $ES; return $ES ? $es : $en; }

$cfg = file_exists($cfgfile) ? parse_ini_file($cfgfile) : [];
function v($cfg, $k, $d = "") { return htmlspecialchars($cfg[$k] ?? $d); }

/* Field help is written as plain markdown blockquotes below each field. Unraid
   turns every blockquote into <blockquote class="inline_help">, hidden by CSS
   and unfolded by the built-in Help button in the top bar. Use that mechanism —
   do not build a custom one. */

$status   = json_decode(shell_exec("bash " . escapeshellarg($script) . " status 2>/dev/null"), true) ?: [];
$switched = ($status['switched'] ?? 'no') === 'yes';
$watcher  = $status['watcher'] ?? 'stopped';
$lastrep  = (int)($status['last_replicate'] ?? 0);

/* Health as last seen by the watcher, not a fresh probe: showing it live would
   make this page wait several seconds every time it loads, and it would also
   disagree with what the watcher is actually acting on. */
$health   = $status['health'] ?? '';
$fails    = (int)($status['fails'] ?? 0);
$checked  = (int)($status['checked'] ?? 0);
$age      = $checked > 0 ? time() - $checked : -1;

/* A reading nobody has refreshed is not a reading. If the watcher died, the last
   value on disk could be hours old and would be read as if it were current. */
$stale    = ($age < 0 || $age > 120);

switch ($health) {
    case '0': $hlabel = t('HEALTHY', 'SANO');            $hclass = 'fg-ok';   break;
    case '1': $hlabel = t('DOWN', 'CAÍDO');              $hclass = 'fg-bad';  break;
    case '2': $hlabel = t('INCONCLUSIVE', 'NO CONCLUYENTE'); $hclass = 'fg-warn'; break;
    default:  $hlabel = t('unknown', 'desconocido');     $hclass = 'fg-warn'; break;
}
if ($stale) { $hlabel = t('no recent reading', 'sin lectura reciente'); $hclass = 'fg-warn'; }

/* The standby is shown next to the origin on purpose. A failover system where
   nobody ever looks at plan B is how a replica ends up broken for days. */
$target = $status['target'] ?? 'off';
?>

<style>
.fg-bar   { display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; align-items:center; gap:8px; margin-bottom:16px; }
.fg-hero  { font-size:1.25em; font-weight:bold; padding:10px 16px; border-radius:5px; color:#fff; }
.fg-state { padding:6px 10px; border-radius:4px; color:#fff; }
.fg-ok    { background:#1a7f37; }
.fg-warn  { background:#9a6700; }
.fg-bad   { background:#b32d2e; }
.fg-note  { opacity:.7; font-size:.9em; }
/* Not inline_help: this one is a live warning about the current state, so it has
   to be visible without pressing anything. */
.fg-warnbox { margin:0 0 14px 0; padding:8px 12px; border-left:3px solid #9a6700;
              background:rgba(128,128,128,.12); line-height:1.45; max-width:760px; }
</style>

<div class="fg-bar">
  <span class="fg-hero <?= $hclass ?>"><?= t('Origin', 'Origen') ?>: <?= $hlabel ?></span>
  <?php if (!$stale && $fails > 0): ?>
    <span class="fg-state fg-warn"><?= t('Consecutive failures', 'Fallos seguidos') ?>: <?= $fails ?>/<?= v($cfg,'FAIL_THRESHOLD','3') ?></span>
  <?php endif; ?>
  <?php if ($target !== 'off'): ?>
    <span class="fg-state <?= $target === 'ok' ? 'fg-ok' : 'fg-bad' ?>">
      <?= t('Standby copy', 'Copia en espera') ?>: <?= $target === 'ok' ? t('healthy','sana') : t('BROKEN','ROTA') ?>
    </span>
  <?php endif; ?>
  <span class="fg-state <?= $switched ? 'fg-warn' : 'fg-ok' ?>">
    <?= $switched ? t('FAILED OVER — serving locally', 'CONMUTADO — sirviendo en local')
                  : t('Normal — the origin is serving', 'Normal — sirve el origen') ?>
  </span>
  <span class="fg-state <?= $watcher === 'running' ? 'fg-ok' : 'fg-bad' ?>">
    <?= t('Watcher', 'Vigilante') ?>: <?= $watcher === 'running' ? t('running','en marcha') : t('stopped','parado') ?>
  </span>
  <?php if ($lastrep > 0): ?>
    <span class="fg-state fg-ok"><?= t('Last replica', 'Última réplica') ?>: <?= date('d/m/Y H:i', $lastrep) ?></span>
  <?php endif; ?>
  <?php if (!$stale): ?>
    <span class="fg-note"><?= t('checked', 'comprobado hace') ?> <?= $age ?> <?= t('seconds ago', 'segundos') ?></span>
  <?php endif; ?>
</div>

<?php if ($health === '2' && !$stale): ?>
<div class="fg-warnbox">
<?= t('This server has no internet access, so the origin cannot be judged from here. No failover will happen while this lasts — and it would be pointless anyway, since nobody outside could reach this box either.',
      'Este servidor no tiene salida a Internet, así que desde aquí no se puede juzgar el origen. No se conmutará mientras dure — y tampoco serviría de nada, porque nadie de fuera podría llegar a este servidor.') ?>
</div>
<?php endif; ?>

<!-- /update.php is what actually writes the file. /update.htm is only the
     progress page, so posting there looks like it worked and saves nothing. -->
<form markdown="1" method="POST" action="/update.php" target="progressFrame">
<input type="hidden" name="#file" value="<?= $plugin ?>/config">

**<?= t('Enable failover', 'Activar el failover') ?>:**
: <select name="ENABLED">
  <option value="no"  <?= v($cfg,'ENABLED','no')=='no'  ? 'selected' : '' ?>><?= t('No','No') ?></option>
  <option value="yes" <?= v($cfg,'ENABLED','no')=='yes' ? 'selected' : '' ?>><?= t('Yes','Sí') ?></option>
  </select>

> <?= t('The master switch. While this is off the watcher stays stopped and nothing is ever switched: no DNS changes, no proxy host enabled, no data copied. Everything else on this page is only read when this is on.',
                   'El interruptor principal. Mientras esté en No, el vigilante no arranca y no se conmuta nada: ni se toca el DNS, ni se habilita el proxy, ni se copian datos. El resto de la página solo se usa cuando está en Sí.') ?>

### <?= t('Origin', 'Origen') ?>

**<?= t('Origin host', 'Host del origen') ?>:**
: <input type="text" name="ORIGIN_HOST" value="<?= v($cfg,'ORIGIN_HOST') ?>" placeholder="vps.example.com">

> <?= t('The machine being watched: your VPS or remote server, by its own host name — for example vps.example.com. <b>Not</b> your public domain. If your domain goes through Cloudflare or any CDN, that domain answers even when the server behind it is dead, so watching it would tell you nothing.',
                   'La máquina que se vigila: tu VPS o servidor remoto, por su propio nombre de host — por ejemplo vps.example.com. <b>No</b> tu dominio público. Si el dominio pasa por Cloudflare o cualquier CDN, ese dominio responde aunque el servidor de detrás esté muerto, así que vigilarlo no diría nada.') ?>

**<?= t('TCP ports to probe', 'Puertos TCP a sondear') ?>:**
: <input type="text" name="ORIGIN_PORTS" value="<?= v($cfg,'ORIGIN_PORTS','443') ?>" placeholder="22 443">

> <?= t('Ports that should be open when the server is alive, separated by spaces. Any one of them answering counts as alive. 22 (SSH) and 443 (HTTPS) is a good pair: if both are silent, the machine is really gone.',
                   'Puertos que deberían estar abiertos con el servidor vivo, separados por espacios. Basta con que responda uno para darlo por vivo. 22 (SSH) y 443 (HTTPS) es una buena pareja: si los dos callan, la máquina no está.') ?>

**<?= t('Health path', 'Ruta de salud') ?>:**
: <input type="text" name="HEALTH_PATH" value="<?= v($cfg,'HEALTH_PATH') ?>" placeholder="/api/health">

> <?= t('Optional, but it is what makes this reliable. A URL path on your site that answers something predictable, such as /api/health. An open port only proves the machine booted — the web server or the database can be dead behind it and the port stays open. This is requested straight at the origin by IP, so a CDN in front cannot answer for it. Leave it empty and only ports are checked.',
                   'Opcional, pero es lo que hace esto fiable de verdad. Una ruta de tu web que responda algo predecible, por ejemplo /api/health. Un puerto abierto solo demuestra que la máquina arrancó: el servidor web o la base de datos pueden estar muertos detrás y el puerto sigue abierto. Esto se pide directamente al origen por IP, así que un CDN delante no puede responder por él. Si lo dejas vacío, solo se comprueban los puertos.') ?>

**<?= t('Expected in response', 'Se espera en la respuesta') ?>:**
: <input type="text" name="HEALTH_EXPECT" value="<?= v($cfg,'HEALTH_EXPECT') ?>" placeholder='"ok":true'>

> <?= t('A piece of text that must appear in that response for the site to count as healthy. It does not have to be the whole answer, just something that only appears when things work. If you leave it empty, any non-empty response counts.',
                   'Un fragmento de texto que debe aparecer en esa respuesta para dar el sitio por sano. No hace falta que sea la respuesta entera, basta con algo que solo salga cuando todo va bien. Si lo dejas vacío, vale cualquier respuesta que no venga vacía.') ?>

### <?= t('Switching', 'Conmutación') ?>

**<?= t('Domain', 'Dominio') ?>:**
: <input type="text" name="DOMAIN" value="<?= v($cfg,'DOMAIN') ?>" placeholder="example.com">

> <?= t('The domain whose DNS gets switched, and it must be a zone in your Cloudflare account. Every A and CNAME record in that zone is repointed, so use a domain dedicated to this site, not one shared with other services.',
                   'El dominio cuyo DNS se conmuta, y tiene que ser una zona de tu cuenta de Cloudflare. Se reapuntan <b>todos</b> los registros A y CNAME de esa zona, así que usa un dominio dedicado a este sitio, no uno compartido con otros servicios.') ?>

**<?= t('Failover target', 'Destino del failover') ?>:**
: <input type="text" name="FAILOVER_TARGET" value="<?= v($cfg,'FAILOVER_TARGET') ?>" placeholder="home.example.com">

> <?= t('Where the domain points during an outage: this Unraid box, seen from the internet. A host name becomes a CNAME record, an IP becomes an A record. If your home connection has a changing IP, use the dynamic DNS name you already keep updated, with a short TTL — the plugin checks it matches your current public IP before switching, and refuses if it does not.',
                   'A dónde apunta el dominio durante una caída: este servidor Unraid, visto desde internet. Un nombre de host se pone como registro CNAME; una IP, como registro A. Si tu conexión de casa cambia de IP, usa el nombre de DNS dinámico que ya mantienes al día, con TTL corto — el plugin comprueba que coincide con tu IP pública actual antes de conmutar, y se niega si no coincide.') ?>

**<?= t('Cloudflare API token', 'Token de API de Cloudflare') ?>:**
: <input type="password" name="CF_TOKEN" value="<?= v($cfg,'CF_TOKEN') ?>">

> <?= t('Created at Cloudflare under My Profile -> API Tokens. Give it <b>Zone:Read</b> and <b>DNS:Edit</b>, and restrict it to this one zone. Do not use a Global API Key: that one can do anything to your whole account, and this plugin only needs to edit these records.',
                   'Se crea en Cloudflare, en Mi perfil -> Tokens de API. Dale <b>Zone:Read</b> y <b>DNS:Edit</b>, y limítalo a esta única zona. No uses la Global API Key: esa puede hacer cualquier cosa en toda tu cuenta, y este plugin solo necesita editar estos registros.') ?>

**<?= t('Check interval (s)', 'Intervalo de comprobación (s)') ?>:**
: <input type="number" name="CHECK_INTERVAL" min="5" max="600" value="<?= v($cfg,'CHECK_INTERVAL','10') ?>">

> <?= t('How often the origin is checked, in seconds. 10 is a good default: it is frequent enough to notice an outage almost immediately and light enough that the origin barely notices.',
                   'Cada cuánto se comprueba el origen, en segundos. 10 es un buen valor: lo bastante frecuente para enterarse casi al momento y lo bastante ligero para que el origen ni lo note.') ?>

**<?= t('Failures before switching', 'Fallos antes de conmutar') ?>:**
: <input type="number" name="FAIL_THRESHOLD" min="1" max="20" value="<?= v($cfg,'FAIL_THRESHOLD','3') ?>">

> <?= t('How many checks in a row must fail before switching. Detection time is interval x this number — 10 seconds x 3 means about half a minute. Do not set it to 1: a single dropped packet would move your live site.',
                    'Cuántas comprobaciones seguidas deben fallar antes de conmutar. El tiempo de detección es intervalo × este número: 10 segundos × 3 son unos treinta segundos. No lo pongas a 1: un solo paquete perdido movería tu web en producción.') ?>

### <?= t('Nginx Proxy Manager (optional)', 'Nginx Proxy Manager (opcional)') ?>

**<?= t('NPM data path', 'Ruta de datos de NPM') ?>:**
: <input type="text" name="NPM_DATA" value="<?= v($cfg,'NPM_DATA') ?>" placeholder="/mnt/user/appdata/NginxProxyManager/data">

> <?= t('The folder you mapped to /data in your Nginx Proxy Manager container — the one holding database.sqlite. Leave this section empty if you do not use NPM; then only DNS is switched and you take care of serving the site yourself.',
                   'La carpeta que tienes mapeada a /data en tu contenedor de Nginx Proxy Manager, la que contiene database.sqlite. Deja esta sección vacía si no usas NPM: entonces solo se conmuta el DNS y de servir el sitio te encargas tú.') ?>

**<?= t('NPM container', 'Contenedor de NPM') ?>:**
: <input type="text" name="NPM_CONTAINER" value="<?= v($cfg,'NPM_CONTAINER','Nginx-Proxy-Manager-Official') ?>">

> <?= t('The container name exactly as it appears on the Docker tab. It is used to reload nginx after enabling or disabling the host.',
                   'El nombre del contenedor tal cual aparece en la pestaña Docker. Se usa para recargar nginx después de habilitar o deshabilitar el host.') ?>

**<?= t('Proxy host ID', 'ID del proxy host') ?>:**
: <input type="text" name="NPM_HOST_ID" value="<?= v($cfg,'NPM_HOST_ID') ?>" placeholder="65">

> <?= t('The numeric id of the proxy host in NPM that serves this site from your Unraid box. Open that host in the NPM interface and read the number at the end of the address bar. <b>Leave that host disabled</b> in normal times: the plugin enables it only during a failover, so two copies of your site never accept writes at the same time.',
                   'El número identificador del proxy host de NPM que sirve este sitio desde tu Unraid. Abre ese host en la interfaz de NPM y mira el número al final de la barra de direcciones. <b>Deja ese host deshabilitado</b> en condiciones normales: el plugin lo habilita solo durante un failover, para que nunca haya dos copias de tu sitio aceptando escrituras a la vez.') ?>

### <?= t('Standby copy', 'Copia en espera') ?>

**<?= t('Standby health URL', 'URL de salud de la copia') ?>:**
: <input type="text" name="TARGET_HEALTH_URL" value="<?= v($cfg,'TARGET_HEALTH_URL') ?>" placeholder="http://192.168.100.62/api/health">

> <?= t('Optional but strongly recommended: a URL where <b>the copy on this box</b> answers, checked every few minutes so you find out that plan B is broken <b>before</b> you need it, not during the outage. Point it straight at the backend (the container address), not at the public domain: while the proxy host is disabled — which is the correct resting state — the site is not reachable here by its public name. Leave empty to skip this check.',
                   'Opcional pero muy recomendable: una URL donde responda <b>la copia de este servidor</b>, que se comprueba cada pocos minutos para que te enteres de que el plan B está roto <b>antes</b> de necesitarlo, no durante la caída. Apúntala directamente al backend (la dirección del contenedor), no al dominio público: mientras el proxy host está deshabilitado — que es el reposo correcto — el sitio no es alcanzable aquí por su nombre público. Déjala vacía para no hacer esta comprobación.') ?>

**<?= t('Expected in the response', 'Texto esperado en la respuesta') ?>:**
: <input type="text" name="TARGET_EXPECT" value="<?= v($cfg,'TARGET_EXPECT') ?>" placeholder='"ok":true'>

> <?= t('Text the standby answer must contain. Leave empty to accept any non-empty answer — but a page that returns an error message with a 200 status would pass that way, which is exactly the failure this check exists to catch.',
                   'Texto que debe contener la respuesta de la copia. Déjalo vacío para aceptar cualquier respuesta no vacía — pero así pasaría una página que devuelve un mensaje de error con estado 200, que es justo el fallo que esta comprobación existe para detectar.') ?>

**<?= t('Standby check interval (s)', 'Intervalo de comprobación (s)') ?>:**
: <input type="number" name="TARGET_CHECK_INTERVAL" min="30" value="<?= v($cfg,'TARGET_CHECK_INTERVAL','300') ?>">

> <?= t('How often the standby is asked, in seconds. It does not need the origin\'s pace: a copy breaks slowly, through a bad sync or a permissions change, not from one second to the next. Only changes are notified, so a copy that stays broken does not keep alerting you.',
                   'Cada cuánto se pregunta a la copia, en segundos. No necesita el ritmo del origen: una copia se rompe despacio, por una sincronización mala o un cambio de permisos, no de un segundo para otro. Solo se avisa de los cambios, así que una copia que sigue rota no te avisa una y otra vez.') ?>

### <?= t('Data hooks', 'Enganches de datos') ?>

**<?= t('Replicate hook', 'Enganche de réplica') ?>:**
: <input type="text" name="HOOK_REPLICATE" value="<?= v($cfg,'HOOK_REPLICATE') ?>" placeholder="/boot/config/plugins/failover-guard/replicate.sh">

> <?= t('Path to a script of yours that copies the live data from the origin down to this box, so the local copy is ready to take over. It runs while everything is fine. This plugin never touches your data: only you know whether that means a database, a folder, or both.',
                   'Ruta a un script tuyo que copia los datos del origen hasta este servidor, para que la copia local esté lista para tomar el relevo. Se ejecuta mientras todo va bien. El plugin no toca tus datos: solo tú sabes si eso es una base de datos, una carpeta o las dos cosas.') ?>

**<?= t('Pre-failback hook', 'Enganche previo al failback') ?>:**
: <input type="text" name="HOOK_PRE_FAILBACK" value="<?= v($cfg,'HOOK_PRE_FAILBACK') ?>" placeholder="/boot/config/plugins/failover-guard/failback.sh">

> <?= t('Path to a script of yours that copies data <b>from this box back up to the origin</b>, before handing the site back. This is the important one: while the domain pointed here, this is where new data was written, so pulling from the origin instead would erase it. It runs with the local proxy host already disabled, so nothing can be written mid-copy, and if it fails the failback is cancelled and this box keeps serving.',
                   'Ruta a un script tuyo que copia los datos <b>desde este servidor de vuelta al origen</b>, antes de devolverle el sitio. Este es el importante: mientras el dominio apuntó aquí, es aquí donde se escribieron los datos nuevos, así que traerlos del origen los borraría. Se ejecuta con el proxy local ya deshabilitado, para que nada pueda escribirse a mitad de copia, y si falla se cancela el failback y este servidor sigue sirviendo.') ?>

**<?= t('Replicate interval (s)', 'Intervalo de réplica (s)') ?>:**
: <input type="number" name="REPLICATE_INTERVAL" min="300" value="<?= v($cfg,'REPLICATE_INTERVAL','3600') ?>">

> <?= t('How often the replicate hook runs, in seconds. 3600 is once an hour. Whatever happened since the last copy is what you would lose in an outage, so shorter means less loss and more load on the origin.',
                   'Cada cuánto se ejecuta el enganche de réplica, en segundos. 3600 es una vez por hora. Lo ocurrido desde la última copia es lo que perderías en una caída, así que menos tiempo significa perder menos y cargar más el origen.') ?>

**<?= t('Retry wait (s)', 'Espera entre reintentos (s)') ?>:**
: <input type="number" name="RETRY_INTERVAL" min="10" value="<?= v($cfg,'RETRY_INTERVAL','60') ?>">

> <?= t('A hook may refuse to run — typically because the site is busy right now and copying would capture a half-written state. When that happens the copy stays pending and is retried, and this is how long to wait between those retries. Without it the retry would happen on every pass of the watcher, hammering the origin for as long as the site stays busy.',
       'Un enganche puede negarse a ejecutarse, normalmente porque el sitio está en uso justo en ese momento y copiar capturaría un estado a medio escribir. Cuando eso pasa, la copia queda pendiente y se reintenta, y esto es lo que se espera entre reintentos. Sin ello el reintento ocurriría en cada vuelta del vigilante, machacando el origen mientras el sitio siga en uso.') ?>

**<?= t('Notifications', 'Notificaciones') ?>:**
: <select name="NOTIFY">
  <option value="yes" <?= v($cfg,'NOTIFY','yes')=='yes' ? 'selected' : '' ?>><?= t('Yes','Sí') ?></option>
  <option value="no"  <?= v($cfg,'NOTIFY','yes')=='no'  ? 'selected' : '' ?>><?= t('No','No') ?></option>
  </select>

> <?= t('Send an Unraid notification when a failover or failback happens, or when one is refused. These are rare and always worth reading, so leaving this on is recommended.',
                   'Envía una notificación de Unraid cuando ocurre un failover o un failback, o cuando se rechaza uno. Son raras y siempre merece la pena leerlas, así que lo recomendable es dejarlo activado.') ?>

&nbsp;
: <input type="submit" value="<?= t('Apply','Aplicar') ?>"><input type="button" value="<?= t('Done','Hecho') ?>" onclick="done()">

</form>

### <?= t('Checks', 'Comprobaciones') ?>

<pre id="fgtest" style="max-height:260px;overflow:auto"><?= t('Press the button to run every check without changing anything.',
                                                              'Pulsa el botón para ejecutar todas las comprobaciones sin cambiar nada.') ?></pre>

<input type="button" value="<?= t('Run checks','Comprobar') ?>" onclick="fgTest()">

### <?= t('Log', 'Registro') ?>

<pre id="fglog" style="max-height:420px;overflow:auto"><?= t('Loading…','Cargando…') ?></pre>

<input type="button" value="<?= t('Refresh','Actualizar') ?>" onclick="fgLog()">
<input type="button" value="<?= t('Clear','Vaciar') ?>" onclick="fgClear()">
<label><input type="checkbox" id="fgauto" onchange="fgAuto()"> <?= t('Auto-refresh (5s)','Auto-actualizar (5s)') ?></label>
<select id="fglines" onchange="fgLog()">
  <option value="100">100</option>
  <option value="200" selected>200</option>
  <option value="500">500</option>
  <option value="2000">2000</option>
</select>

> <?= t('Full file','Fichero completo') ?>: `/var/log/<?= $plugin ?>.log`

<script>
var fgTimer = null;
var fgRunning = <?= json_encode(t('Running…','Comprobando…')) ?>;

function fgTest() {
  document.getElementById('fgtest').textContent = fgRunning;
  $.post('/plugins/<?= $plugin ?>/include/action.php', {action:'test'}, function(d) {
    document.getElementById('fgtest').textContent = d;
  });
}

function fgLog() {
  var n = document.getElementById('fglines').value;
  $.post('/plugins/<?= $plugin ?>/include/action.php', {action:'log', lines:n}, function(d) {
    var box = document.getElementById('fglog');
    // Keep the newest lines in view: that is what you came to read.
    var stuck = (box.scrollTop + box.clientHeight >= box.scrollHeight - 30);
    box.textContent = d;
    if (stuck) box.scrollTop = box.scrollHeight;
  });
}

function fgClear() {
  $.post('/plugins/<?= $plugin ?>/include/action.php', {action:'clearlog'}, function() { fgLog(); });
}

function fgAuto() {
  if (document.getElementById('fgauto').checked) {
    fgTimer = setInterval(fgLog, 5000);
  } else {
    clearInterval(fgTimer); fgTimer = null;
  }
}

$(function() {
  fgLog();
  document.getElementById('fglog').scrollTop = document.getElementById('fglog').scrollHeight;
});
</script>
]]>
 </INLINE>
 <SHA256>0c82e3d25fd60b67fc758ffa1849cccb75005ddd0a9ca912e001e9d43e67add4</SHA256>
</FILE>

<FILE Name="/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/failover-guard/include/action.php" Mode="0644">
 <INLINE>
<![CDATA[
<?php
/* Actions triggered from the settings page.
   Only read-only checks are exposed here on purpose: switching over is the
   watcher's job, and a stray click should never move a live site. */

$script = "/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/failover-guard/scripts/failover-guard";
$logfile = "/var/log/failover-guard.log";
$action = $_POST['action'] ?? '';

switch ($action) {
    case 'test':
        header('Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8');
        echo shell_exec("bash " . escapeshellarg($script) . " test 2>&1");
        break;

    case 'status':
        header('Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8');
        echo shell_exec("bash " . escapeshellarg($script) . " status 2>&1");
        break;

    case 'log':
        header('Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8');
        if (!file_exists($logfile)) { echo "No log yet / Todavía no hay registro"; break; }
        $lines = (int)($_POST['lines'] ?? 200);
        if ($lines < 20)   $lines = 20;
        if ($lines > 2000) $lines = 2000;
        echo shell_exec("tail -n $lines " . escapeshellarg($logfile) . " 2>&1");
        break;

    case 'clearlog':
        header('Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8');
        file_put_contents($logfile, "");
        echo "Log cleared / Registro vaciado";
        break;

    default:
        http_response_code(400);
        echo "unknown action";
}
]]>
 </INLINE>
 <SHA256>af7c9ae9479f79b391992b45029e29189d45a76e61e3fffa997e38c394de2f62</SHA256>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
mkdir -p &cfgdir;/state

# Seed a config on first install so the page has something to read.
if [ ! -f &cfgdir;/config ]; then
  cat &gt; &cfgdir;/config &lt;&lt;'CFG'
ENABLED="no"
ORIGIN_HOST=""
ORIGIN_PORTS="443"
HEALTH_PATH=""
HEALTH_EXPECT=""
DOMAIN=""
FAILOVER_TARGET=""
CF_TOKEN=""
CHECK_INTERVAL="10"
FAIL_THRESHOLD="3"
NPM_DATA=""
NPM_CONTAINER="Nginx-Proxy-Manager-Official"
NPM_HOST_ID=""
TARGET_HEALTH_URL=""
TARGET_EXPECT=""
TARGET_CHECK_INTERVAL="300"
HOOK_REPLICATE=""
HOOK_PRE_FAILBACK=""
REPLICATE_INTERVAL="3600"
RETRY_INTERVAL="60"
BOOT_GRACE="300"
NOTIFY="yes"
CFG
  chmod 600 &cfgdir;/config
fi

# The watcher is a long-lived process; this keeps it alive across crashes,
# array restarts and plugin updates.
cat &gt; /etc/cron.d/&name; &lt;&lt;'CRON'
*/5 * * * * root /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/failover-guard/scripts/supervise >/dev/null 2>&amp;1
CRON
[ -x /usr/local/sbin/update_cron ] &amp;&amp; /usr/local/sbin/update_cron

&plugdir;/scripts/supervise >/dev/null 2>&amp;1 || true

echo ""
echo "----------------------------------------------------"
echo " failover-guard has been installed."
echo " Configure it at Settings -&gt; Failover Guard."
echo " Version: &version;"
echo "----------------------------------------------------"
echo ""
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="remove">
<INLINE>
PID=$(cat &cfgdir;/state/watcher.pid 2>/dev/null)
[ -n "$PID" ] &amp;&amp; kill "$PID" 2>/dev/null
rm -f /etc/cron.d/&name;
[ -x /usr/local/sbin/update_cron ] &amp;&amp; /usr/local/sbin/update_cron
rm -rf &plugdir;
echo ""
echo " failover-guard removed. Settings kept in &cfgdir;"
echo ""
</INLINE>
</FILE>

</PLUGIN>
